<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hassan al-Banna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hassan Ahmed Abd al-Rahman Muhammed al-Banna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hassan al-Banna was an Egyptian schoolteacher and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-brotherhood">Muslim Brotherhood</a>, one of the largest and most influential Islamist movements. Born in Mahmudiyya in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, he trained at Dar al-Ulum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and taught at a primary school in Ismailia, where in March 1928 six workers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> Company made him their leader. He held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to be a comprehensive system of life and criticized Western materialism and British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>. By the late 1930s the Brotherhood had branches in every Egyptian province, and a decade later 500,000 active members. After the government outlawed it in December 1948, he was shot in Cairo and died on 12 February 1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hassan-al-banna.jpg" alt="Hassan al-Banna" width="330" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hassan_al-Banna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1928 <b>Hassan al-Banna</b> founded the Muslim Brotherhood in Ismailiyah, Egypt, arguably the first, largest and most influential modern Islamic political and religious organisation.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
